    "description": "Say which stage of the work you are in, for the user's progress pane. The pane already infers stages from your tool calls (edits, test commands, review agents, git and gh); call this when that would be wrong or too coarse: when you start a stage the calls do not show (planning in prose, an adversarial review by hand), or to count progress inside one (\"fixing review findings\", done 3, total 10). One short call; it changes nothing else."
   },
   {
    "name": "diagram_get",
    "description": "Read what the user's diagram side pane shows: the live work map, the diagrams pinned from your replies, and which box the user has opened. Each comes back as mermaid with stable node ids; a subgraph is a box with a detail diagram inside that the user can open. Call this when the user talks about \"the diagram\", \"the map\", a box or a pin, and before diagram_set. The user can change diagrams from the pane too, so read again instead of trusting an earlier copy."
   },
   {
    "name": "diagram_set",
    "description": "Change a diagram in the user's side pane. target: \"view\" (what the user is looking at; the default), \"map\" (the live work map) or \"pin:N\" (a pinned diagram, N as diagram_get lists it). path: node ids to go down through from that diagram's top level (from the open box for \"view\"); the change applies to the last node's detail, created if it has none. Give mermaid (a whole flowchart that replaces that level: ids you keep keep their details, subgraph blocks become details the user can open) and/or ops (small patches). The pane then shows the changed level. Edits stay: the automatic map refresh builds on them instead of redrawing over them."
   }
